Ezero examples

Compile a source with the native original Zero command:
    ezero examples/hello.ezero NEW_OUTPUT
Then run the output on its target system. The toolkit command and lib directory
must remain together. Linux, Windows and Zero OS use the same frontend and
runtime; guest inline source and filesystem capacities are bounded separately.
See ../README.ztext, ../HOST.ztext and ../../portability/guest/ABI.ztext.

hello.ezero / hello.zero: identical three-line output through two languages.
banana.ezero: unquoted UTF-8 text, a typed phrase and one-based indexing.
values.ezero: inferred values, reassignment, text concatenation, conditions.
lists.ezero: iteration, accumulation, indexing and nested mixed values.
functions.ezero: parameters, returned text and recursive local call frames.
memory.ezero: temporary objects reclaimed while retained values survive.
host-files.ezero: native arguments and exclusive creation of a new text file.
files.ezero: create, replace, read, search, volume roots and explicit deletion.
text.ezero: LF/CRLF lines, one-based selection and joining text lists.

File examples use raw file contents with extensionless names; they do not
claim to encode ZTEXT001. The original zero-text tool manages that checked
document format. File examples modify only their named destinations. files.ezero accepts an
optional native directory argument; choose an empty directory for exploration.
No example manually allocates memory, traverses character pointers, declares a
value type or invokes a foreign runtime. Compiled programs contain their own
instruction data and the original Zero runtime.
